# CoW view-materialization on the GENERIC EQUIP METHOD body-lowering path.
#
# AXIS CELL — path = `lower_equip_method_with_subs`
# (src/ir/lowering/functions.rs). `Cell[T]` holds a `Vector[T]`; `probe(&self)`
# binds a view of element 0 and then calls `self.resize(v)`, which pushes 64
# times and reallocates. One of eleven function-body-lowering paths; the five
# per-function CoW / auto-move prescans used to be hand-copied into only two of
# them, so this one lowered with an EMPTY `cow_reassigned_after`: the mark was
# made and the materialize still did not happen, and the view dangled.
#
# ⚠ THE CONTROL IS THE POINT, AND IT IS WHAT NAMED THE DEFECT. Hand-monomorphise
# this file — `Cell[T]` -> `Cell`, `T` -> `String`, nothing else — and it was
# rc 0 printing `helloworld` even before the fix, because a NON-generic equip
# method lowers through `lower_equip_method`, one of the two paths that already
# ran the prescans. ONE type parameter was the whole difference, and the
# difference was the PATH, not the genericness: a generic FREE function
# (`cow_generic_fn_view_survives_realloc.gg`), a trait DEFAULT method
# (`cow_trait_default_view_survives_realloc.gg`), a static trait method, a
# closure body and the four module-loop bodies all crashed on the same shape.
#
# ⚠ NOT the "mutator NAME decides memory safety" class: this was rc 139 under
# `resize` (a name that WAS on the retired `MUTATING_METHODS` list, so the
# prescan DID mark the receiver) and under `grow` (a name that was not). The
# mark was made and the materialize still did not happen — the break was
# DOWNSTREAM of the classification, at the per-function-body entry.
#
# THE REALLOC IS LOAD-BEARING (64 pushes; one push is rc 0 and proves nothing).
# The heap payload via `mk` is NOT load-bearing for reproduction — it is kept so
# the freed element is a heap String and ASan reports a legible
# heap-use-after-free rather than a bare SIGSEGV.
#
# RED-VERIFIED against the pre-fix compiler: rc 139 SIGSEGV on C AND LLVM, ASan
# `heap-use-after-free`. ggdef, the definitional interpreter, adjudicated
# `helloworld` all along — both compilers were wrong and the oracle knew.
String mk(String a, String b):
    return a + b

struct Cell[T]:
    Vector[T] items

equip Cell[T]:
    void resize(&self, T v):
        int i = 0
        while i < 64:
            self.items.push(v)
            i = i + 1

    T probe(&self, T v):
        T s = self.items.get(0).unwrap()
        self.resize(v)
        return s

void main():
    Cell[String] h = Cell[String]([mk("hello", "world")])
    print(h.probe(mk("xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx", "yyyyyyyyyyyyyyyy")))
